notes               = "Untitled in source; dated تهران ۱۳۶۸ (Tehran, 1989). هذیان = 'delirium' (fever/shell-shock). Hinges on the symmetry نفرت از جنگ / نفرت از صلح (hatred of war in the eyes, of peace in the teeth). Shares the drained-palette imagery of the page-67 poem and the پناهگاه (shelter) of the page-60 poem. Imported as a draft with machine and lantern layers. Corrected (July 2026): this poem's ===translation=== held an agent rendering, staged there by the pages 61-69 bulk import. Per CLAUDE.md that layer is Ben's alone, so the rendering is now the ===lantern=== and ===translation=== is empty again, superseding the import's earlier and rougher lantern (kept in git history). date_translated is cleared for the same reason — it recorded 6/28/26, the date of the agent pass. **English title 'Shell Shock' is the translator's, at Ben's direction (July 2026) — it is not in the Persian**, which is untitled; هذیان بعد از جنگ is the first line. The cost is worth recording: هذیان recurs four times in the book (the drowsy delirium of the alleys in dark-city, the fevered raving of horse-hooves, talking in one's sleep in letter-alizadeh) and 'shell shock' cannot carry those, so this rendering gains a punch and breaks a thread. Open for Ben: whether هذیان بعد از جنگ... is printed as a title above the poem rather than as line 1 — the transcription treats it as a line, and if it is a title then persian_title should hold it and 'Shell Shock' becomes a translation rather than an invention. Also: the Persian body carries no commas at all and exactly one full stop (after سبز کم رنگ بودند), so the lantern now matches that and lands the last line bare."
draft               = true

===persian===

هذیان بعد از جنگ...
اگر ابرها خاکستری نبودند
باران رنگ دیگری داشت
و اشک‌های تو
سبز کم رنگ بودند.
رنگ جنگ در آه می‌ماند در نگاه می‌ماند
من نفرت از جنگ را در چشم‌ها دیده‌ام
و نفرت از صلح را در دندان‌ها
اگر ابرها خاکستری نبودند
باران رنگ دیگری داشت
می‌دانم چشم‌هایت در مهتاب زیباترند
اما...
باید به پناهگاه برگردیم

تهران ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

Delirium after war...
If the clouds were not gray
The rain would have another color
And your tears
Would be pale green.
The color of war remains in a sigh, remains in a gaze
I have seen the hatred of war in eyes
And the hatred of peace in teeth
If the clouds were not gray
The rain would have another color
I know your eyes are more beautiful in the moonlight
But...
We must return to the shelter

Tehran 1368

===lantern===
Shell shock, after the war...
If the clouds were not grey
the rain would be another colour
and your tears
would be pale green.
The colour of war stays in the sigh stays in the eye
I have seen the hatred of war in eyes
and the hatred of peace in teeth
If the clouds were not grey
the rain would be another colour
I know your eyes are more beautiful in the moonlight
but...
we must go back to the shelter

Tehran, 1368

===translation===

===footnotes===
هذیان is the raving of fever or shell-shock, and بعد از جنگ dates it: ۱۳۶۸ is 1989, a year after the
August 1988 ceasefire. **So there is no raid.** The poem announces itself as a symptom and then
performs one — the counterfactual comes back word for word, the way a delirious man repeats himself,
and the closing line is a reflex firing after the danger has stopped. What breaks off the tenderness
is not a siren but the war still running inside him. پناهگاه is the same bomb-shelter as the page-60
poem, which was written while the shelter was still needed.

The Persian turns to sound at exactly the point where it states its finding. رنگ جنگ (*rang-e jang*,
"the colour of war") rhymes inside itself, and the line then closes on آه … نگاه (*âh … negâh*) —
four rhyming syllables in one line, and not a comma anywhere. English can have one of the two:
*stays in the sigh, stays in the eye*, taking نگاه as the eye rather than the gaze, which the next
line's چشم‌ها is about to do regardless.

چشم‌ها and دندان‌ها are bare plurals — "in eyes," "in teeth," with no possessive. Not *their* eyes,
not *our* teeth: an anatomy, and a diagnosis. Eyes are where a thing is received and teeth are where
it is bared, so the body is split, one half sick of the war and the other half unable to stop. That
someone should hate *the peace*, a year after it arrived, is the coldest line in the book.

And the colour. خاکستری, grey, is this poet's own — "I love the greys, the greys…" (page 76), grey as
red and green cancelling each other (page 88), a lifetime's grey road (pages 104–105). Here it is the
enemy: grey cloud makes grey rain makes grey tears, and the counterfactual is a painter complaining
about his palette. The one colour he reaches for is سبز کم‌رنگ, pale green — and green in this book is
longing (page 88, "the green of longing"). Her tears would be longing, if the sky would let them.
